        "id": 1962300
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-09-05T12:53:50Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027d prefer to implement this in lib.rs so we don\u0027t need to store GATT state in bluetooth.rs (decouple them). Something like:\n```\nMessage::ProfileDisconnected(device_address) \u003d\u003e {\n    if bluetooth_gatt.lock().unwrap().connected_clients(device_address) \u003d\u003d vec![bas_uuid]\n        \u0026\u0026 !bluetooth.lock().unwrap().is_hh_connected(device_address)\n        \u0026\u0026 !bluetooth_socketmgr.lock().unwrap().is_device_connected(device_address)\n        \u0026\u0026 bluetooth_media.lock().unwrap().get_connected_profiles(device_address).is_empty() {\n        bluetooth_gatt.lock().unwrap().drop_device(device_address);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThen, in each of them (bluetooth/_gatt/_socketmgr/_media) we just propagate this event whenever there\u0027s a profile disconnected.",
      "revId": "9e8f43f59cecf9e3cc1615c3cb1a8707717ba3d8",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6c9b4fa0_c809ca27",
        "filename": "system/gd/rust/linux/stack/src/bluetooth.rs",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 1305,
      "author": {
        "id": 1936647
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-09-05T16:56:27Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I will go ahead and implement this, but my personal opinion is that this would contradict our existing system architecture. Currently, `lib.rs` just handles dispatching messages between modules in the stack, while all logic about what actions to take is handled by the modules themselves. Moving the disconnect logic into `lib.rs` breaks that precedent.\n\nGetting to the specifics of the suggestion: what exactly is the ideal outcome? My understanding was \"If a GATT connection is dropped, determine whether we should disconnect the device entirely\". However, it seems from this comment that what we actually want is \"If any service/profile/connection is dropped, potentially drop the GATT connection\". Does that seem more accurate?",
      "parentUuid": "ba43f476_308306e5",
      "revId": "9e8f43f59cecf9e3cc1615c3cb1a8707717ba3d8",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "10fc06b7_a60f7f1c",
        "filename": "system/gd/rust/linux/stack/src/bluetooth.rs",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 1305,
      "author": {
        "id": 1962300
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-09-06T02:42:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e `lib.rs`\n\nAgree that we shouldn\u0027t put heavy logic in `lib.rs` (this case seems acceptable to me though). I have no strong opinion to move this to `lib.rs` now, but I still prefer not to store GATT state in `bluetooth.rs`. I guess we can just query the GATT state from `bluetooth_gatt`. And for socket manager you\u0027ll need to get it from `lib.rs`\n\n\u003e what we actually want is \"If any service/profile/connection is dropped, potentially drop the GATT connection\". Does that seem more accurate?\n\nYes. Consider an LE mouse or LE audio device. If they disconnect only the main profiles but not ACL for some reason (perhaps because BAS was connected from our end, Smart Audio Source Switch, etc), then the connection would never be dropped. Therefore verifying the status on every profile connection changed seems preferable to me.",
      "parentUuid": "6c9b4fa0_c809ca27",
      "revId": "9e8f43f59cecf9e3cc1615c3cb1a8707717ba3d8",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
